    {
      "id": "-cr-mWAZS4u6OFlKI1L68",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 572.8303418576395,
      "y": 1378.46012224876,
      "width": 335.43975830078125,
      "height": 25,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aw",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1924525334,
      "version": 55,
      "versionNonce": 441475158,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787095854328,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) Geo-indexing : Spatial structure",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) Geo-indexing : Spatial structure",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "NAgoX8F3VHiRtTo_BsGAO",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 572.2840843781939,
      "y": 1425.9335984516204,
      "width": 958.479248046875,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ax",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1897528714,
      "version": 318,
      "versionNonce": 262573130,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787096067515,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "i) Geohash: \n- Encode lat/lng into a base32 string; shared prefix ≈ nearby area\n- Simple, easy to shard by prefix, works with any key-value store\n- Cells are rectangular, not circular (radius mismatch); precision jumps are coarse; boundary problem\nii) S2 cells (Google S2):\n- Hierarchical cells on a cube-sphere projection   \n- Handles poles/antimeridian correctly, more uniform cell area globally, still hierarchical/shardable\n- More complex to implement/reason about than geohash           \niii) Quad-tree:\n- Recursive spatial subdivision, adaptive to data density\n- Naturally denser index where entities are denser (cities)    \n- Harder to shard cleanly across machines than a flat prefix scheme   \niv) R-tree (PostGIS)\n- Bounding-box tree over actual geometries\n- Off-the-shelf via PostGIS /KNN, support arbitrary polygons\n- Less natural to horizontally shard yourself; you're relying on the DB's internals  \n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "i) Geohash: \n- Encode lat/lng into a base32 string; shared prefix ≈ nearby area\n- Simple, easy to shard by prefix, works with any key-value store\n- Cells are rectangular, not circular (radius mismatch); precision jumps are coarse; boundary problem\nii) S2 cells (Google S2):\n- Hierarchical cells on a cube-sphere projection   \n- Handles poles/antimeridian correctly, more uniform cell area globally, still hierarchical/shardable\n- More complex to implement/reason about than geohash           \niii) Quad-tree:\n- Recursive spatial subdivision, adaptive to data density\n- Naturally denser index where entities are denser (cities)    \n- Harder to shard cleanly across machines than a flat prefix scheme   \niv) R-tree (PostGIS)\n- Bounding-box tree over actual geometries\n- Off-the-shelf via PostGIS /KNN, support arbitrary polygons\n- Less natural to horizontally shard yourself; you're relying on the DB's internals  \n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "oTYYAK_7UjAAP88yK8lIE",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 563.7184762175657,
      "y": 1934.6307211795763,
      "width": 2947.978515625,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ay",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1029218518,
      "version": 55,
      "versionNonce": 452446934,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1787096065609,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": " Geohash                     | Encode lat/lng into a base32 string; shared prefix ≈ nearby area | Simple, easy to shard by prefix, works with any key-value store                                     | Cells are rectangular, not circular (radius mismatch); precision jumps are coarse; boundary problem (see below) |\n| S2 cells (Google S2)        | Hierarchical cells on a cube-sphere projection                   | Handles poles/antimeridian correctly, more uniform cell area globally, still hierarchical/shardable | More complex to implement/reason about than geohash                                                             |\n| Quad-tree                   | Recursive spatial subdivision, adaptive to data density          | Naturally denser index where entities are denser (cities)                                           | Harder to shard cleanly across machines than a flat prefix scheme                                               |\n| R-tree (e.g., PostGIS GiST) | Bounding-box tree over actual geometries                         | Off-the-shelf via PostGIS `ST_DWithin`/KNN, supports arbitrary polygons                             | Less natural to horizontally shard yourself; you're relying on the DB's internals                               |\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": " Geohash                     | Encode lat/lng into a base32 string; shared prefix ≈ nearby area | Simple, easy to shard by prefix, works with any key-value store                                     | Cells are rectangular, not circular (radius mismatch); precision jumps are coarse; boundary problem (see below) |\n| S2 cells (Google S2)        | Hierarchical cells on a cube-sphere projection                   | Handles poles/antimeridian correctly, more uniform cell area globally, still hierarchical/shardable | More complex to implement/reason about than geohash                                                             |\n| Quad-tree                   | Recursive spatial subdivision, adaptive to data density          | Naturally denser index where entities are denser (cities)                                           | Harder to shard cleanly across machines than a flat prefix scheme                                               |\n| R-tree (e.g., PostGIS GiST) | Bounding-box tree over actual geometries                         | Off-the-shelf via PostGIS `ST_DWithin`/KNN, supports arbitrary polygons                             | Less natural to horizontally shard yourself; you're relying on the DB's internals                               |\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ce7ZS4hLh9DW4F994hazJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 695.6909302045481,
